==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|bekenen}}, {{m|enm|beknen}}, {{m|enm|becnen}}, {{m|enm|beknien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|bēacnian}}, {{m|ang|bēcnian}}, {{m|ang|bīecnan||to signal; beckon}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*bauknōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*bauknijaną||to signal}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*baukną||signal; beacon}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|bōknian}}, {{cog|goh|bouhnen}}, {{cog|non|bákna}}. More at {{l|en|beacon}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɛkən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkən}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[wave]] or [[nod]] to [[somebody]] with the [[intention]] to [[make]] the [[person]] [[come]] [[closer]].
#* {{rfdatek|en|Dryden}}
#*: His distant friends, he '''beckons''' near.
#* {{rfdatek|en|Shakespeare}}
#*: It '''beckons''' you to go away with it.
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[seem]] [[attractive]] and [[inviting]]

